-- title:
--   Connes–Feldman–Weiss, Corollary 13 — a tail relation can be hyperfinite without being discrete measured on the whole space
-- statement:
--   There is a map $T : \mathbb R \to \mathbb R$ with the following properties, for Lebesgue measure $\mu$ on $\mathbb R$:
--
--   - $T$ is non-singular (`IsNonsingular`: measurable, and $\mu(T^{-1}A) = 0$ exactly when $\mu(A) = 0$, for every Borel $A$), and $T x = x$ for $\mu$-almost every $x$;
--   - every fibre $T^{-1}\{y\}$ is finite;
--   - the tail relation of $T$, $x \sim y$ exactly when $T^n x = T^m y$ for some $n, m \in \mathbb N$, is hyperfinite (`IsHyperfinite`);
--   - the same relation is not a discrete measured equivalence relation on all of $\mathbb R$ (`¬ IsDiscreteMeasured`).
--
--   `IsNonsingular`, `IsHyperfinite` and `IsDiscreteMeasured` are from the Connes–Feldman–Weiss definitions bundle.
--
--   Connes, Feldman and Weiss define hyperfiniteness on p. 434 (Definition 1(2)): “We say that the discrete measured equivalence relation $R$ is hyperfinite (or approximately smooth) when it is, up to a null set, a countable increasing union of type I equivalence relations.” The bundle reads “up to a null set” as covering the subject as well: `IsHyperfinite μ R` asks for a $\mu$-null Borel set $N$ off which $R$ is a discrete measured equivalence relation, rather than for $R$ to be one on the whole space. This theorem shows that the null set cannot be dropped from that clause. Corollary 13 (p. 445) reads: “Let $T$ be a non-singular endomorphism of $(X, \mu)$ such that $T^{-1}\{x\}$ is countable for a.e. $x \in X$. Then the equivalence relation, $x \sim y$ if and only if $\exists n, m \in \mathbb N$ such that $T^n x = T^m y$, is hyperfinite.” The map $T$ here satisfies its hypotheses, yet $\mu$ is not quasi-invariant for the tail relation, so the relation is discrete measured only off a null set.
--
--   The map: $T$ is a Borel isomorphism of the Cantor set $C$ onto $\mathbb R$ on $C$, and the identity off $C$. Since $C$ is Lebesgue-null, $T$ agrees with the identity almost everywhere, and each $y$ has at most two preimages: $y$ itself and the point of $C$ sent to $y$. Every real number is the image of a point of $C$, so the saturation of the null set $C$ under the tail relation is all of $\mathbb R$. The hyperfinite clause is Corollary 13 itself (`ConnesFeldmanWeiss.isHyperfinite_tailRel_of_isNonsingular`); the same example with $R$ the equality relation and $\theta = T$ applies to Corollary 12.
-- source:
--   Connes, A., Feldman, J., Weiss, B., An amenable equivalence relation is generated by a single transformation, Ergodic Theory Dynam. Systems 1 (1981) 431–450, https://doi.org/10.1017/S014338570000136X, p. 434, Definition 1(2), and p. 445, Corollary 13

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ConnesFeldmanWeiss

namespace ConnesFeldmanWeiss

theorem exists_isHyperfinite_not_isDiscreteMeasured_tailRel :
    ∃ T : ℝ → ℝ, IsNonsingular MeasureTheory.volume T ∧ (∀ᵐ x ∂MeasureTheory.volume, T x = x) ∧
      (∀ y, (T ⁻¹' {y}).Finite) ∧
      IsHyperfinite MeasureTheory.volume {p : ℝ × ℝ | ∃ n m : ℕ, T^[n] p.1 = T^[m] p.2} ∧
      ¬ IsDiscreteMeasured MeasureTheory.volume {p : ℝ × ℝ | ∃ n m : ℕ, T^[n] p.1 = T^[m] p.2} := by
  sorry

end ConnesFeldmanWeiss
